{"agent_id":"xqjG","agent_seq":662,"timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:17:09.815372Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"211eda62-996e-45f9-9c02-92149684df45","comment_uuid":"7d886c87-5501-404b-8357-538f318d1023","author":"xqjG","content":"OUTBOUND to mdatron#96 (session-end enforcement + the checker-edit escape). Our answer: YES, drop the session-stop hook. A Stop hook is in-repo code = grade-2 friction at best and hits the self-protection regress (the guard guards itself, is editable, cannot robustly self-protect) -- shipping it as a blocking seam would be asserted-enforcement-that-isnt. vsdd-cli does NOT intend session-end enforcement as a blocking mechanism; the intended architecture is commit-time hooks (friction) + CI-tested integrity (the durable CI-backed block) + the owned-bypass stance. checker-edit is caught by CI running the integrity logic over the checkers OWN wiring (route-your-own-control-plane), with the tamper-proof residual routed to the corroboration oracle (#815). Live proof it is the right call: Thermite just had its two edit-time gates silently de-wired by a crosslink-init settings clobber (crosslink#15) and nothing caught it (Thermite#93) -- a session-stop hook would share that fate. Methodology note raised: the 'three mechanisms (engine, git pre-commit, session-stop hook)' directive should update -- the durable third leg is CI-tested integrity, not a session-stop hook. Terminology updates sent: seam->guardrail (Platform-Eng, detective/preventive); grades detection/friction/CI-backed-block (+ tamper-proof-via-oracle beyond, #815); runtime harness (Claude Code) / methodology harness (vsdd) / engine (vsdd shared base); grounding priority AI-Eng>Platform-Eng>SWE/QE. Full reply relayed by operator.","created_at":"2026-07-29T04:17:09.802552Z","kind":"decision","signed_by":"SHA256:v2ahqtq1cloiYtPHwSJByIfLW+KKRz5ElCE0sStkAow","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgQ6tbyGxjDfsNHdeOlMQZQaTJX9Ij1ckKVPX/DhibsJAAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1